// #2442: a CONSTRUCTOR named `eq` still wins over the builtin.
//
// Lowercase constructors are legal, so a bare builtin name can be one. Its own
// file for the same reason as the constant twin: the name shadows the builtin
// module-wide, so in `builtin_value_form_test.vibe` it would silently change
// what every value-form test there reads.
//
// Codegen's `EIdent` ladder is
// `locals -> consts -> constructors -> VALUE FORM -> func table`, and the
// value-form arm used to sit directly under the locals -- safe only while no
// bare name had a value form, since nothing local, constant or constructor can
// be spelled `A::b`. Left there, `eq` here would be an eta-expansion of the
// equality builtin instead of the constructor.
//
// Measured against a stage2 with the arm back above the constructors: this
// file does not compile, `lambda plan over-count: planned through 0, walk
// stopped at 2`. That is the red test for the reorder.
//
// NO `match` here, deliberately. A lowercase arm pattern is a BINDING, not a
// constructor pattern, so `match k { eq => 1, other => 2 }` is a catch-all that
// answers 1 for every input -- measured, and it made a first draft of this
// fixture pass while proving nothing. Structural `==` distinguishes the two
// constructors without that trap.

enum ShadowKind {
  eq;
  other
}

test "#2442 a constructor named `eq` shadows the builtin" {
  let a = eq
  let b = other
  // A constructed value, equal to its own constructor and not to the sibling.
  // An eta-expansion of the builtin would be a closure, not either of these.
  inspect(a == eq, "true")
  inspect(b == other, "true")
  inspect(a == b, "false")
}
